    "summary": "Monumental Lovelight jam, strong second‑set jams, historic MIT vibe",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a towering 31‑minute Turn On Your Lovelight and tight Easy Wind and Candyman performances. Jerry’s guitar shines throughout while the band builds solid energy in the second set.",
    "review": "May 7, 1970 at MIT remains one of those early‑‘70 concerts that feels both raw and transcendent. The night opens with a modest first set – a mix of folk‑rock numbers and some uneven moments – but the real magic erupts once the band dives into the electric second set. Good Lovin' bursts out, segues into an extended drum break, then rolls into a string of high‑energy jams: Cold Rain And Snow, Dire Wolf, Easy Wind and the unforgettable 31‑minute Turn On Your Lovelight that closes the night. Jerry Garcia’s humor (“The Eleven”) and his steel‑guitar cameo with the New Riders add extra sparkle, while Mickey Hart’s beat‑calling drives the groove. Though audience recordings show typical ’70 tape quirks – hiss, occasional EQ wobble and chatty mic intrusions – the historic atmosphere and stellar improvisations more than compensate, making this MIT show a must‑listen for fans who crave that early Dead fire.\n\nThe performance is not without its rough edges; several songs in the first set (Rider, Speedway, Black Peter, Casey Jones, King Bee) stumble, reflecting the band’s occasional inconsistency at the time. Still, the standout moments – especially the Lovelight marathon and the tight interplay on Easy Wind and Candyman – cement this concert as a key snapshot of the Dead’s evolving live prowess.",
